title: 'Warpline M1c shipped — `warpline restore`: a working tree reconstructed with git ABSENT (the layer→VCS threshold)'
summary: 'Built and shipped M1c (T-2026-07-01-012): the `warpline restore` verb. Warpline now reconstructs a byte-identical working tree — contents, exec bits, symlinks, subdirs — from its own native object store with .git DELETED. Proven end-to-end (I ran rm -rf .git && restore myself; diff IDENTICAL). Selector grammar HEAD/@seq/pick:/state:/tree: → strand → binding.treeId; A4 refuses a strand with no byte binding; dirty-dest guard. Security-critical: hardened restoreTree closes Aegis''s C3 (per-level single-component name validation, reject .git/.warpline at every level, no symlink-follow, fail-closed on forged trees) — Aegis adversarial review verdict C3 CLOSED, SHIP 0.85. His review surfaced two adjacent gaps I reproduced LIVE and fixed in the same commit: a --force HARDLINK write-through that clobbered a file outside dest (now breaks the link with a fresh inode), and an unvalidated tree:<id> reaching the object-store path (now shape-validated); also added an unknown-mode fail-closed. This crosses the layer→VCS threshold the readiness panel scored ~0.05 on: Warpline can now OWN and REPRODUCE bytes without git. 233/233 tests green. Commit bc5f1bc5 (local, unpushed).'
symbols_touched:
  - '#restore'
  - '#warpline-cli'
  - '#warp-store'
  - '#materialize'
  - '#select'
  - '#strand'
learnings:
  - 'The ''verify with your own eyes'' discipline paid off twice this milestone: (1) the git-absent restore is real, confirmed by an independent rm-rf-.git run, not just a green test; (2) re-running the reviewer''s findings as LIVE exploits confirmed BOTH the hardlink write-through and the tree:-id store-escape were real (the reviewer could only static-trace) — fixed and regression-tested before cementing. A read-only reviewer''s ''MED, needs live repro'' is a to-do, not a footnote.'
  - 'M1 is now functionally COMPLETE across its wedge: native object store (M1a) + durable merge bytes (M1b) + authenticated fabric (M1.9 schema-v2) + git-absent restore (M1c). Warpline owns and reproduces bytes without git — the capability the readiness panel scored ~0.05 on now exists and is proven.'
  - 'Sequencing the authenticated fabric (M1.9) BEFORE restore (M1c) was correct: restore now materializes bytes bound to a tamper-evident pickId chain, so the restore path sits on an authenticated store rather than a forgeable one — exactly the reorder the audit argued for.'
